            "text": "Before the city wakes, its objects seem to belong to themselves. A bicycle leans against a bakery wall, keeping its own counsel. Traffic lights change for empty streets. Somewhere, a delivery driver rests both hands around a paper cup, borrowing a little warmth before the business of the day begins.\n\nThis is my favorite imaginary hour: not quite night, not yet morning, when every window holds a different shade of blue. Even the pigeons look thoughtful. They step around yesterday\u2019s crumbs with the solemn concentration of scholars examining a ruined civilization, occasionally discovering that history tastes faintly of sesame seeds.\n\nSoon enough, everything will acquire a purpose. The bicycle will carry someone late to work. The bakery will sell its first loaf. The driver will navigate a difficult intersection while an impatient voice crackles through a speaker. For now, though, the world has not finished putting on its public face.\n\nThere is something merciful about that unfinishedness. We spend so much time being legible: answering messages, explaining decisions, making ourselves useful or interesting or easy to understand. An empty street asks for none of this. You can simply walk through it, carrying thoughts that have not yet learned their names.\n\nThen a door opens. A broom whispers across a threshold. The first bus sighs at the curb, bright inside as a traveling kitchen. Someone laughs, and the sound makes the morning official. Nothing extraordinary has happened. Still, for a moment, the ordinary seems less like a category than a gift."
          }
